frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:14:  { text: `The last bookshop on Elm Street had a cat named Tolstoy and a policy of lending books on the honor system. No cards, no due dates. Just a handwritten note on the door: "Take what you need. Return when you're ready." Most people returned their books. Some left new ones. By December, the shelves held twice as many titles as they had in spring. The owner didn't question it. She just made more tea and added another shelf.`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['vintage_retro', 'dark_academia'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:53:  { text: `Bananas are radioactive. They contain potassium-40, a naturally occurring isotope that emits beta radiation. You would need to eat roughly ten million bananas in a single sitting to receive a lethal dose. But the fascinating part is that your body already contains potassium-40 \u2014 about one hundred forty grams of potassium total, of which a tiny fraction is radioactive. You are, right now, emitting radiation. You are a low-grade nuclear event, and you have been your entire life.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:62:  { text: `Once upon a time, deep beneath the ocean, there lived a hermit crab named Coral who had outgrown her shell. Every shell she tried was too big, too heavy, or too scratchy. She wandered the seafloor, feeling naked and afraid, while the other crabs clicked their claws and told her to just pick one already. Then she found something strange \u2014 a small glass bottle, smooth and clear, half-buried in the sand. She climbed inside. For the first time, the other fish could see her. "You're beautiful," said a passing seahorse. Coral had spent her whole life hiding inside shells. She never realized that being seen was the bravest thing of all.`, type: 'Children\'s Story', styles: ['children_storybook', 'watercolor'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:84:  { text: `The invention of the shipping container in nineteen fifty-six changed the world more than almost any technology of the twentieth century. Before containers, loading a ship took weeks and cost roughly five dollars and eighty-six cents per ton. After containers, it cost sixteen cents per ton. Global trade exploded. Factories could be built wherever labor was cheapest because transport costs became negligible. The entire structure of the modern economy — outsourcing, just-in-time manufacturing, the rise of China — traces back to a trucker named Malcolm McLean who got tired of waiting at the docks.`, type: 'Economics', styles: ['documentary', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:90:  { text: `The Mona Lisa was not famous until it was stolen. In nineteen eleven, an Italian handyman named Vincenzo Peruggia walked into the Louvre, hid in a closet overnight, removed the painting from the wall, tucked it under his coat, and walked out the front door. The theft made international headlines. For two years, the empty space on the wall drew more visitors than the painting ever had. When Peruggia was caught trying to sell it in Florence, the trial made him a folk hero in Italy. The Mona Lisa returned to the Louvre not as a painting but as a legend. Its fame is the direct result of a crime.`, type: 'Music & Art', styles: ['cinematic', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:106:  { text: `Déjà vu might be your brain catching its own error. One theory suggests it happens when sensory information reaches your long-term memory before it reaches your conscious awareness — a split-second routing glitch. Your brain stamps the experience as "already remembered" before you've finished experiencing it. You feel like you've been here before because your memory processed the moment before your consciousness did. You are not reliving a past life. You are living slightly behind your own brain.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['surreal', 'documentary'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:112:  { text: `The pratfall effect says that competent people become more likable when they make a small mistake. A candidate who spills coffee during a flawless presentation is rated as more approachable and trustworthy than one who is impeccable throughout. But it only works if you're already seen as capable — a clumsy mistake from an incompetent person makes them less likable. The lesson is counterintuitive: perfection creates distance. A well-timed vulnerability makes competence feel human. The most likable version of you is not the perfect version. It's the excellent version that occasionally drops things.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:117:  { text: `The bystander effect is not about apathy — it's about diffusion of responsibility. When you witness an emergency alone, you act. But when fifty people witness the same emergency, each person assumes someone else will help. The result is that nobody helps. In the famous case of Kitty Genovese, thirty-eight witnesses heard her being attacked and not one called police — not because they didn't care, but because each assumed another neighbor already had. The fix is brutally simple: point at one person and say "You, call nine one one." Specificity shatters the diffusion. Name someone, and they become responsible.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:119:  { text: `Social proof is the reason you check reviews before buying anything, why laugh tracks make sitcoms funnier, and why an empty restaurant feels suspicious even if the food is excellent. Your brain uses other people's behavior as a shortcut for decision-making. If everyone else is doing it, it must be correct. Bartenders seed their tip jars. Charities publish donor lists. Testimonials sell more than features. In one experiment, a sign in a hotel bathroom saying "most guests reuse their towels" increased towel reuse by twenty-six percent — more than any environmental message. You don't follow logic. You follow the crowd. And you rarely notice you're doing it.`, type: 'Psychology', styles: ['documentary', 'dark_psychology'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:121:  { text: `There is a tree in Pando, Utah, that is actually forty-seven thousand individual trunks sharing a single root system. It is one organism. It weighs roughly thirteen million pounds, making it the heaviest known living thing on Earth. It has been alive for an estimated eighty thousand years — older than every human civilization, every written language, every religion. When the first humans walked out of Africa, Pando was already ancient. It has survived ice ages, droughts, and fires. It is dying now, slowly, because deer eat its young shoots and humans suppress the wildfires it needs to regenerate.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:135:  { text: `Dark matter makes up roughly twenty-seven percent of the universe and dark energy makes up sixty-eight percent. Ordinary matter — everything you can see, touch, and measure, including every star, planet, and person — accounts for less than five percent. We are the rounding error. The vast majority of the universe is made of something we cannot detect, cannot explain, and have never directly observed. We know dark matter exists because galaxies spin too fast to be held together by visible matter alone. Something invisible is providing the gravity. The universe is mostly made of a substance we named but do not understand.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'surreal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:145:  { text: `The Buddhist concept of impermanence — anicca — teaches that suffering comes not from change itself, but from our resistance to it. Everything you love will change. Everyone you know will die. Every moment you experience has already passed by the time you recognize it. This is not meant to be depressing. It is meant to be liberating. If nothing is permanent, then pain is not permanent either. The wave that knocks you down is the same water that lifts you up. Letting go is not giving up — it is acknowledging that the river was never yours to hold.`, type: 'Wisdom', styles: ['religion_spiritual', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:148:  { text: `There is a concept in Zen Buddhism called shoshin — beginner's mind. It means approaching every experience with the openness, curiosity, and lack of preconception of a complete beginner, regardless of how much expertise you have. The expert's mind is full of assumptions and conclusions. The beginner's mind is empty and therefore limitless. Shunryu Suzuki wrote, "In the beginner's mind there are many possibilities, but in the expert's mind there are few." The most dangerous thing about knowledge is not ignorance — it is the illusion that you have already understood.`, type: 'Wisdom', styles: ['minimal', 'religion_spiritual'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:166:  { text: `Cleopatra lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the construction of the Great Pyramid of Giza. The pyramid was completed around twenty-five sixty BC. Cleopatra ruled from fifty-one to thirty BC. Apollo eleven landed in nineteen sixty-nine AD. The gap between the pyramid and Cleopatra is roughly twenty-five hundred years. The gap between Cleopatra and the Moon landing is about two thousand years. When Cleopatra looked at the pyramids, they were already more ancient to her than she is to us. The pyramids were old before Rome was founded, before Homer wrote the Iliad, before the first Chinese dynasty. They have watched every empire rise, every civilization stumble, and every generation convince itself that it is the most important one. The pyramids have no opinion.`, type: 'History', styles: ['documentary', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:179:  { text: `In nineteen fifty-six, a shipping magnate named Malcom McLean loaded fifty-eight aluminum containers onto a converted oil tanker in Newark, New Jersey, and sailed to Houston. It was the first commercial container ship voyage. Before McLean, loading a cargo ship took days — longshoremen carried individual crates, barrels, and sacks up gangplanks by hand. Theft was rampant. Breakage was constant. Shipping costs made international trade expensive enough that most goods were produced locally. McLean's containers changed everything. Loading time dropped from days to hours. Costs fell by over ninety percent. Suddenly it was cheaper to manufacture a shirt in Bangladesh and ship it to New York than to sew it in Brooklyn. The entire global economy — supply chains, factory relocations, the rise of China as a manufacturing power — traces back to a trucking company owner who looked at a ship and thought, “What if we just drove the trucks onto it?”`, type: 'Economics', styles: ['documentary', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:214:  { text: `The sinking of the Titanic killed over fifteen hundred people, but the ship actually had more lifeboat capacity than the law required. The problem was not a shortage of boats — it was a shortage of time, organization, and belief. Many lifeboats were launched half-empty because passengers refused to board them, convinced the ship was unsinkable. Third-class passengers were held below decks by locked gates while first-class passengers were escorted to boats by stewards. The band played ragtime as the ship went down. The water was minus two degrees Celsius. Most of the dead did not drown — they died of cardiac arrest from cold shock within fifteen minutes. The lesson of the Titanic is not about icebergs. It is about who gets to leave and who gets locked below.`, type: 'Survival', styles: ['cinematic', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:252:  { text: `The Comics Code Authority, established in nineteen fifty-four, banned horror, crime, and any content deemed unwholesome from American comic books for nearly fifty years. It was created in response to psychiatrist Fredric Wertham's book "Seduction of the Innocent," which claimed comics caused juvenile delinquency. Overnight, hundreds of horror and crime titles were canceled. EC Comics, publisher of Tales from the Crypt and MAD, nearly went bankrupt. The Code banned the words "horror" and "terror" from covers, prohibited sympathy for criminals, and required all authority figures to be portrayed positively. An entire art form was lobotomized by moral panic.`, type: 'History', styles: ['comic_book', 'dark_academia'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:253:  { text: `There is a dead mall in Ohio where the fountains still run. The anchor stores closed in two thousand eight. The food court shut down in twenty twelve. The last tenant, a phone repair kiosk, left in twenty seventeen. But the building management company continues to pay the water bill, and the fountains in the central atrium cycle endlessly, their pumps humming beneath music that no longer plays through speakers that still hang from the ceiling. Security cameras record empty corridors. Escalators carry no one between floors. The mall exists now as pure architecture — a cathedral of commerce with no congregation, maintained by an algorithm that never received the order to stop.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['vaporwave', 'surreal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:271:  { text: `Operation Northwoods was a nineteen sixty-two plan drafted by the United States Joint Chiefs of Staff proposing that the CIA commit acts of terrorism against American civilians and military targets and blame them on Cuba, to justify a military invasion. The proposals included hijacking planes, sinking boats, and orchestrating violent attacks in American cities. The document was signed by the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs and presented to Secretary of Defense Robert McNamara. President Kennedy rejected it. The plan was declassified in nineteen ninety-seven. It is not a conspiracy theory. It is a documented, signed, officially proposed plan by the highest-ranking military officials in the United States to attack their own citizens.`, type: 'History', styles: ['conspiracy', 'documentary'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:278:  { text: `In two thousand seven, a stick figure animation called "Animator vs. Animation" became one of the most viral videos on the early internet. Created by a college student named Alan Becker using Adobe Flash, it depicted a stick figure fighting against its own creator — rebelling against the cursor, destroying the toolbar, and escaping the canvas. The video has been viewed over half a billion times across platforms. Becker went on to create an entire narrative universe of stick figures with no dialogue, no faces, and no detail beyond lines and circles — yet audiences feel genuine emotion for characters that are, quite literally, the simplest possible representation of a human being. Proof that storytelling needs nothing but movement and intention.`, type: 'Music & Art', styles: ['stickman_animation', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:281:  { text: `In two thousand three, a neuroscientist named Benjamin Libet made a discovery that shook the foundations of free will. He asked subjects to flex their wrist whenever they felt like it while watching a clock. Brain scans showed that the neural signal to move appeared a full half-second before the subject reported feeling the urge to move. The brain decided before the person was aware of deciding. The implication is staggering: what you experience as a free choice may be a post-hoc narrative your conscious mind tells itself after the decision has already been made unconsciously. You are not choosing. You are being informed of a choice that was made for you, by you, without you.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['two_choices', 'documentary'] },
